Compiles. Now write tests. Test plan:

Test 1: `AgingServiceTest` (@SpringBootTest with H2, real DB) — main flows:
- full happy path: create batch (storedAt fixed), 7 days turns + temps, release → success, snapshot contains 7 entries, view shows release.
- day4 missing temp → release 422 with missingTemperatureDates contains day4; then add valid temp for day4 → release succeeds.
- out-of-range temp → release 422 with that date.
- duplicate turn same day different requestId → 409.
- same requestId same params replay → same record id; different params → 409.
- release idempotent replay same requestId → same release id; different requestId after release → 409.
- failed release doesn't consume requestId: fail with reqX, fix data, retry reqX → success.
- void temperature: void a valid temp → replacement appended; if replacement out of range, release fails for that date; void idempotent replay; void after release doesn't change snapshot (release record snapshot unchanged).
- concurrent release: two threads different requestIds → exactly one release_record; other gets 409. Use CountDownLatch, timeout.

Also repository-level unique constraint is exercised via service tests (real H2).

For concurrency test with @SpringBootTest and Hikari pool size 5, two threads calling service.release simultaneously — each in its own transaction. H2 in-memory shared (DB_CLOSE_DELAY=-1, same db name, same JVM → same database). Both read valid state, both insert → unique(batch_id) → one DuplicateKeyException → 409. Good.

Test data isolation: use unique batch codes/requestIds per test (e.g., UUID suffix) to avoid cleanup; or @Sql cleanup. I'll use unique suffixes per test method — simpler and avoids cross-test interference. Use a counter/uuid.

Clock: use fixed clock via @TestConfiguration? Service uses Instant.now(clock) for timestamps only; dates are explicit. Default systemUTC clock is fine. But "时间测试用可控时钟" — I'll add a @MockBean Clock? Simpler: import a test config with a fixed Clock bean as @Primary. Let me add @TestConfiguration with Clock.fixed. That makes turned_at deterministic. I'll do that.

For the web layer, a @WebMvcTest or full MockMvc test for 409/422 status codes? The service throws BusinessException with HttpStatus; the handler maps. A @SpringBootTest + MockMvc (auto-configured) test would verify HTTP statuses. I'll write one web test class with MockMvc covering: create batch, turn, temp, release flow statuses (201, 409, 422), and the day-4 scenario end-to-end via HTTP. That also satisfies "合成业务调用及预期结果".

DB for tests: src/test/resources/application.yaml? Main application.yaml already defaults to H2. For tests use a separate named db: jdbc:h2:mem:cheese-aging-test. Add src/test/resources/application.yaml overriding datasource url. spring.sql.init mode always → schema.sql applied. Good.

Now write tests. Keep each file < ~200 lines.

Test class 1: AgingServiceTest (@SpringBootTest). Methods:

```java
@SpringBootTest
class AgingServiceTest {
    @Autowired AgingService service;
    @Autowired AgingRepository repository;
    @Autowired JdbcTemplate jdbc;

    static final Instant STORED = Instant.parse("2026-01-10T08:30:00Z");
    static final LocalDate DAY1 = LocalDate.of(2026,1,10);

    String suffix() { return UUID.randomUUID().toString().substring(0,8); }

    AgingBatch newBatch() { return service.createBatch("req-batch-"+suffix, "LOT-"+suffix, STORED); }

    void turnDay(AgingBatch b, int day) { service.confirmTurn(b.id(), "req-turn-"+day+"-"+suffix, DAY1.plusDays(day-1), "op"); }
    void tempDay(AgingBatch b, int day, String c) { service.recordTemperature(b.id(), "req-temp-"+day+"-"+suffix, DAY1.plusDays(day-1), new BigDecimal(c)); }
    void fillAllDays(AgingBatch b) { for i 1..7 { turnDay; tempDay "10.0"; } }
```

Tests:
1. happyPath_releaseSucceeds_andSnapshotFrozen: fill, release, assert status RELEASED, snapshot has 7 turnRecordIds; then void a temp used in snapshot; re-read release record → snapshot unchanged; view shows release.
2. missingDay4Temperature_blocksThenPasses: turns all 7, temps except day4 → release 422, details missingTemperatureDates contains 2026-01-13; then record day4 temp → release OK. Also assert failed release didn't consume requestId: use same requestId for both attempts.
3. outOfRangeTemperature_blocks: day3 temp 15.0 → 422 contains date; then void it with replacement 9.0 → release passes.
4. duplicateTurnSameDay_conflict: turn day1 ok; second turn day1 different requestId → 409. And same requestId same params → returns same id; same requestId different operator → 409.
5. releaseIdempotency: fill, release req R → id1; release req R again → same id; release req R2 → 409.
6. voidTemperature_appendsReplacement_andAffectsEligibility: fill all days; void day2 temp with replacement 20.0 (out of range) → release 422 with day2 date; then void the replacement? Replacement is a new VALID record 20.0; void it with replacement 11.0 → release passes. Also void replay same requestId → same result.
7. concurrentRelease_onlyOneRecord: fill; two threads release with different requestIds; assert exactly one release row, one success one 409. Use ExecutorService, CountDownLatch start, futures with 10s timeout.

For asserting BusinessException status: catch and assert status() == HttpStatus.CONFLICT.

Test class 2: AgingApiTest (@SpringBootTest + @AutoConfigureMockMvc) — HTTP-level: create batch 201, turn 201, replay turn 201 same id, conflicting turn 409, temp 201, release 422 with missing dates JSON, complete data, release 201, view 200 contains releaseId. This gives "一条合成业务调用及预期结果".

Also fixed clock test config: 

```java
@TestConfiguration
static class FixedClockConfig { @Bean @Primary Clock fixedClock() { return Clock.fixed(Instant.parse("2026-01-20T00:00:00Z"), ZoneOffset.UTC); } }
```
Include in service test via inner class (automatically picked up if static nested @TestConfiguration? Nested @TestConfiguration is automatically used). Yes, static nested @TestConfiguration classes are automatically detected.

Note: @SpringBootTest will start full context with H2 — fine, local app context managed by test framework.

Write test files now.

